[Model discretization]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Model
GroupName=Discretization
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Model discretization\Allow number of model parameters to exceed data points]
Display=Allow parameters to exceed data points
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. If set to 0, the number of model parameters is limited to the same as the number of measurements in the dataset.
Visible=1
[Model discretization\Use extended model]
Display=Use extended model
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Allow the model to be finely discretized outside of the area covered by the pseudosection, is it only recommended to set this settings to 0 if you running low on computational resources.
Visible=1
[Vertical discretization]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Vertical
GroupName=Discretization
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Vertical discretization\Thickness of first layer]
Display=Thickness of first layer
Type=Real
Min=0.25
Max=1
Default=0.375
User=0.25
Advanced=0
Description=Thickness of the first (most surface near) layer in the model as a fraction of the unit electrode spacing.
Visible=1
[Vertical discretization\Factor to increase thickness layer with depth]
Display=Layer thickness increase factor
Type=Real
Min=1
Max=1.25
Default=1.1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=Thickness of each model layer in % of thickness of the above layer (1.1 = 10%). Thickness of the first layer is defined in the setting 'Thickness of first layer'.
Visible=1
[Vertical discretization\Factor to increase model depth range]
Display=Model depth range
Type=Real
Min=1
Max=5
Default=1.05
User=2
Advanced=0
Description=To use a model that spans a deeper depth range, you can change the factor to increase model depth range, for example from 1.0 to 1.30 to increase the model depth range by 30%.
Visible=1
[Vertical discretization\Model for increase in thickness of layers]
Display=Model for layer thickness increase
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,2
Default=2
User=2
Advanced=0
Description=0=default 10%, 1=default 25%, 2=user defined (Value is defined under 'Layer thickness increase factor').
Visible=0
[Horizontal discretization]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Horizontal
GroupName=Discretization
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Horizontal discretization\Width of blocks]
Display=Width of blocks
Type=Int
Min=1
Max=15
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=This option allows the user to force the program to use model cells that are wider than one unit electrode spacing for all the layers. It is mainly intended for data collected from mobile surveys where a cable with fixed electrodes positions is dragged along during the survey. The distances between the measurement points are often much less than the spacing between the electrode takeouts. The model block widths can be set to 1 to 15 times the unit electrode spacing.
Visible=1
[Horizontal discretization\Make sure blocks have the same width]
Display=Make blocks equal width
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. If selected all model blocks will have the same width.
Visible=1
[Horizontal discretization\Model refinement]
Display=Model refinement
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0.5,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=The program by default uses a model where the width of the interior model blocks is the same as the unit electrode spacing. This works well in most cases. In situations with large resistivity variations near the ground surface better results can be obtained by using model cells with a width of half the electrode spacing.
Visible=1
[Horizontal discretization\Generate model blocks]
Display=Generate model blocks from sensitivity values
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. If set to 1, this option allocates the arrangement of the blocks in the model using the sensitivity values as a guide. The model created using the sensitivity values increases the width of the blocks near the sides in the deeper parts of the model section in order to increase the sensitivity values of the blocks.
Visible=1
[Forward modelling settings]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Forward modelling
GroupName=Discretization
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Forward modelling settings\Type of mesh]
Display=Vertical mesh size
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,2
Default=0
User=2
Advanced=0
Description=0=Normal,1=Fine,2=Finest. This option allows you to use a finer mesh (in the vertical direction) for the finite-difference or finite-element method. The model for a survey line on the surface normally uses a finer mesh for the top two layers, and a single mesh line for the deeper layers. This option allows the use of a finer mesh for the deeper layers. The apparent resistivity values calculated will be more accurate with a finer mesh, but the computer time and memory required will be greater. The use of a finer mesh can give better results for cases where subsurface resistivity contrasts of greater than 20:1 is expected. This is particularly useful in areas where a low resistivity layer lies below a high resistivity layer.
Visible=1
[Forward modelling settings\Number of nodes between adjacent electrodes]
Display=Horizontal mesh size
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=1,2,4
Default=2
User=4
Advanced=0
Description=Option to set the mesh grid used by the forward modeling program to have 1, 2 or 4 nodes between adjacent electrodes. With 4 nodes per electrode spacing, the calculated apparent resistivity values are more accurate (particularly for large resistivity contrasts). However, the computer time and memory required are correspondingly larger. By default, the program will use the 2 nodes option. In normal surveys with electrodes planted into the ground, the spacing between adjacent electrodes is usually the same as the minimum spacing between two electrodes used in an array. In surveys using a mobile cable system, on land or a streamer in aquatic surveys, the spacing between adjacent electrodes along the survey line can be several times smaller than the minimum spacing between two electrodes used in an array used for the measurements. For such data sets, the '1 node' option can be used to reduce the calculation time and memory required by the forward modeling routine.
Visible=1
[Stopping criteria]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Stopping criteria
GroupName=
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Stopping criteria\Convergence limit for relative change in RMS error in percent]
Display=Convergence limit
Type=Real
Min=0.1
Max=20
Default=5
User=5
Advanced=0
Description=The relative change in the RMS error between 2 iterations. If the change is below selected value, the inversion will stop.
Visible=1
[Stopping criteria\RMS CONVERGENCE LIMIT]
Display=RMS convergence limit
Type=Real
Min=0.01
Max=20
Default=0.1
User=0.1
Advanced=0
Description=This sets the percentage RMS error in the inversion of the apparent resistivity data where the program will stop after the model produce has an RMS error less than this limit.
Visible=1
[Stopping criteria\Number of iterations]
Display=Number of iterations
Type=Int
Min=1
Max=30
Default=15
User=15
Advanced=0
Description=This allows the user to set the maximum number of iterations before the inversion stops.
Visible=1
[Settings]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Settings
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Settings\Calculate model resolution]
Display=Calculate model resolution
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=The model resolution gives a measure of how widely the information from a region of the subsurface is spread out to the surrounding model cells in the inverse model. This calculation will increase the inversion time.
Visible=1
[Settings\Reduce effect of side blocks]
Display=Reduce effect of side blocks
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,2,3
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=0=No, 1=Slight, 2=Severe, 3=Very Severe. This option affects the calculation of the Jacobian matrix values for the model blocks located at the sides and bottom of the model section. Normally, for a block located at the side, the contributions by all the mesh elements associated with the model block are added up right to the edge of the mesh. This gives a greater weight to the side block compared to the interior blocks. In some cases, particularly when the robust inversion option is used, this can result in unusually a high or low resistivity value for the side block. This option limits the contribution of the mesh elements outside the ends of the survey line to the Jacobian matrix values for the side blocks.
Visible=1
[Settings\USE LOGARITHM OF APPARENT RESISTIVITY]
Display=Data space
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,2
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=0=Use logarithmic data space, 2=Use linear data space
Visible=1
[Settings\Use fast Jacobian calculation for dense data sets]
Display=Use fast Jacobian calculation for dense data sets
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=This option uses an optimized routine for calculating the Jacobian matrix values for data sets where the number of data points is much greater than the number of electrodes used in making the measurements. Note for some very sparse data sets, this routine can be even slower than the standard method.
Visible=1
[Settings\USE FINITE ELEMENT METHOD]
Display=Always use Finite-element settings*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=This allows you to use either the finite-difference or finite-element method to calculate the apparent resistivity values. By default, the program will use the finite-difference method which is faster if the data set does not contain topography. If the data set contains topography, the default choice is the finite-element method.
Visible=1
[Settings\Flatness filter type, Include smoothing of model resistivity]
Display=Use smoothing of model resistivities*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=0 = Use smoothing on model changes only, 1= use smoothing directly on model as well.
Visible=1
[Settings\Combined Combined Marquardt and Occam inversion]
Display=Combine Marquardt & smoothness constraints*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=Combining the damped least squares method with the smoothness-constrained method, is intended for use in unusual situations where the data sensitivity values of the model blocks are significantly distorted by large resistivity variations.
Visible=1
[Settings\Local optimization option]
Display=Local optimization option*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=1
Description=Placeholder.
Visible=1
[Settings\Use data compression with Incomplete Gauss-Newton]
Display=Use data compression with Incomplete Gauss-Newton*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=Placeholder.
Visible=0
[Settings\Type of finite-element method]
Display=Type of finite-element method
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=0=Triangular,1=Trapezoidal elements.
Visible=0
[LS solver type]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=LS solver type
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[LS solver type\Type of optimisation method]
Display=Type of optimisation method
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,2
Default=2
User=2
Advanced=0
Description=0=Standard Gauss-Newton,2=Incomplete Gauss-Newton. The Standard Gauss-Newton least-squares method, that calculates an exact solution of the least-squares equation, should be used if the number of data points and/or model cells is small (less than a few thousand). If the number of data points and/or model cells is large, the time taken to solve the least-squares equation could be the most time-consuming part of the inversion process. To reduce the inversion time, an alternative method that calculates an approximate solution of the least-squares equation using the Incomplete Gauss-Newton method can be used.
Visible=1
[LS solver type\Convergence limit for Incomplete Gauss-Newton method]
Display=Convergence limit for Incomplete Gauss-Newton method
Type=Real
Min=0.005
Max=0.05
Default=0.005
User=0.005
Advanced=0
Description=Set the convergence accuracy of the solution. For most data sets, an accuracy of about 1% (i.e. a convergence limit of 0.01
Visible=1
[LS solver type\Type of Jacobian matrix calculation]
Display=Type of LS equation solver
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,2
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=0=Quasi-Newton, 1=Gauss-Newton, 2=Mixed.
Visible=0
[Data/model norm]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Data/model norm
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Data/model norm\Robust data constrain]
Display=Data norm (L1,L2)
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=0=L2, 1=L1. The conventional least-squares method, L2 norm, ('standard data constraint') will attempt to minimize the square of difference between the measured and calculated apparent resistivity values. This method gives reasonable results if the data contains random or 'Gaussian' noise. However, if the data set contains 'outlier' data points, this method is less satisfactory. To reduce the effect of such 'outlier' data points, the L1 norm method ('robust data constraint') inversion method where the absolute difference the measured and calculated apparent resistivity values is minimized can be used
Visible=1
[Data/model norm\Cutoff factor for data constrain]
Display=L1 data norm cutoff factor
Type=Real
Min=0.0001
Max=0.1
Default=0.05
User=0.05
Advanced=0
Description=The cutoff factor controls the degree in which this robust data constrain is used. If a value of 0.05 is used, this means the effect of data points where the differences in the measured and calculated apparent resistivity values are much greater than 5 percent will be greatly reduced.
Visible=1
[Data/model norm\Robust model constrain]
Display=Model norm (L1,L2)
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=0=L2, 1=L1. The conventional smoothness-constrained least squares method also attempts to minimize the square of the changes (L2 model norm) in the model resistivity values. This will produce a model with a smooth variation in the resistivity values. Such a model is more suitable where subsurface resistivity also changes in a smooth manner. However, if the subsurface bodies have sharp boundaries, such as the soil-bedrock interface or massive homogeneous bodies, the conventional least-squares smoothness-constrain method tends to smear the boundaries. If the robust model constrained inversion method (L1 model norm) is used, the program will attempt to minimize the absolute changes in the resistivity values. This constraint tends to produce models with sharp interfaces between different regions with different resistivity values, but within each region the resistivity value is almost constant.
Visible=1
[Data/model norm\Cutoff factor for model constrain]
Display=L1 model norm cutoff factor
Type=Real
Min=0.0001
Max=0.1
Default=0.05
User=0.005
Advanced=0
Description=The cut-off factor controls the degree in which this robust model constrain is used. If a large value is used, for example 1.0, the result is essentially that of the conventional smoothness-constrained least-squares inversion method. If a very small value is used, for example 0.001, the result is close to the true L1-norm inversion method.
Visible=1
[Parameter range]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Parameter range
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Parameter range\LIMIT RESISTIVITY VALUES]
Display=Limit resistivity values
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Limit the resistivity upper and lower limits with the values in the next settings.
Visible=1
[Parameter range\Upper limit factor]
Display=DC upper limit factor
Type=Real
Min=10
Max=50
Default=50
User=50
Advanced=0
Description=For resistivity limitation, in units of average resistivity of previous iterations.
Visible=1
[Parameter range\Lower limit factor]
Display=DC Lower limit factor
Type=Real
Min=0.02
Max=0.1
Default=0.02
User=0.02
Advanced=0
Description=For resistivity limitation, in units of average resistivity of previous iterations.
Visible=1
[Parameter range\Model Chargeability Upper Limit (mV/V) for range]
Display=IP Upper Limit
Type=Real
Min=100
Max=900
Default=900
User=900
Advanced=0
Description=Upper limit for model chargeability in mV/V.
Visible=1
[Parameter range\Model Chargeability Lower Limit (mV/V) for range]
Display=IP Lower Limit
Type=Real
Min=0
Max=50
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=Lower limit for model chargeability in mV/V.
Visible=1
[Long survey lines]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Long survey lines
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Long survey lines\Optimize Jacobian matrix calculation]
Display=Optimize Jacobian matrix
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. This can significantly reduce the calculation time without affecting the accuracy of the values
Visible=1
[Long survey lines\Use fast method to calculate Jacobian matrix]
Display=Use fast method to calculate Jacobian matrix
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Use a fast method to calculate the Jacobian matrix by excluding the model cells that are far away from the electrodes used in an array. The Jacobian matrix values and thus the inversion model will be slightly different from that obtained using the standard method
Visible=1
[Long survey lines\Use sparse inversion method for very long survey lines]
Display=Use sparse inversion method
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Intended for long survey lines with 500 electrodes positions or more. This method will still produce a contiguous model along the entire survey line but takes advantage of the sparse nature of the Jacobian matrix to reduce the number of calculations needed. The results will be slightly different (usually less than 5%) from that obtained using the standard method.
Visible=1
[Reference model]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Reference model*
GroupName=Inversion methodology
Advanced=1
Visible=1
Description=
[Reference model\Use reference model in inversion]
Display=Use reference model*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. A reference model can be used to stabilize the inversion, by limiting extreme resistivity and chargeability values.
Visible=1
[Reference model\Damping factor for reference model]
Display=Damping factor for reference*
Type=Real
Min=0
Max=1
Default=0.01
User=0.01
Advanced=1
Description=A damping factor that controls the degree where the inversion model is 'close' to a reference model is used
Visible=1
[Reference model\Type of reference resistivity]
Display=Type of reference resistivity*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=0 = The reference model will be the average of all measured apparent resistivity values, 1= the reference model will be resistivity model after first iteration.
Visible=1
[Reference model\Use zero reference I.P. model value]
Display=Use zero reference I.P. model*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=1=Yes, 0=No, if 1, a chargeability of zero is used as the reference model for the IP inversion, otherwise the average measured apparent I.P will be used.
Visible=1
[Damping settings]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=DC
GroupName=Damping settings
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Damping settings\Initial damping factor]
Display=Initial damping factor
Type=Real
Min=0.01
Max=1
Default=0.15
User=0.1
Advanced=0
Description=If the data set is very noisy, you should use a relatively larger damping factor (for example 0.3). If the data set is less noisy, use a smaller initial damping factor (for example 0.1). The inversion program automatically reduces the damping factor by about half after each iteration until it reaches the selected minimum value.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Minimum damping factor]
Display=Minimum damping factor
Type=Real
Min=0.001
Max=0.75
Default=0.02
User=0.01
Advanced=0
Description=The inversion program automatically reduces the damping factor by about half after each iteration until it reaches the selected minimum value.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Use higher damping for first layer]
Display=Enable first layer damping
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. The first few layers can show a rippling effect. This can be reduced by using a higher damping factor for the first layer.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Extra damping factor for first layer]
Display=Extra damping factor for first layer
Type=Real
Min=1
Max=100
Default=4
User=4
Advanced=0
Description=Only applies if above setting is on, defines how much extra damping is applied to first layer.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Use higher damping factors at sides of model]
Display=Damping factors for changes in distance between blocks
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=Use higher damping factors for changes in distance between blocks
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Adjust damping factors for distances between the blocks in the model]
Display=Adjust damping factors for changes in distance between blocks
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=Adjust the damping factor for the different layers to take into account changes in the distances between the model cells.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Increase of damping factor with depth]
Display=Increase of damping factor
Type=Real
Min=1
Max=2
Default=1.05
User=1.05
Advanced=0
Description=Since the resolution of the resistivity method decreases exponentially with depth, the damping factor used in the inversion least-squares method is normally also increased with each deeper layer in order to stabilize the inversion process. Normally, the damping factor is increased by 1.05 times with each deeper layer, but you can change it. Use a larger value if the model shows unnatural oscillations in the resistivity values in the lower sections. This will help to suppress the oscillations.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Use automatic calculation for change of damping factor with depth]
Display=Automatic calculation of damping factor with depth
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. You can select the choice to allow the program to determine the value to increase the damping factor with depth automatically. This might be a good choice if the thickness of the layers is much thinner than the default values, for example if you had reduced the unit electrode spacing by half in the data file in order to produce a model with smaller model blocks.
Visible=1
[Damping settings\Optimise damping factor]
Display=Optimise damping factor
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=Instead of automatically decreasing the damping factor by half after each iteration, the 'Optimize damping factor' option allows the program to look for an optimum damping factor.
Visible=0
[IP damping settings]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=IP
GroupName=Damping settings
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[IP damping settings\IP DAMPING FACTOR]
Display=IP damping factor
Type=Real
Min=0.01
Max=1
Default=0.25
User=0.25
Advanced=0
Description=Controls the amplitude of the change in the model IP values allowed after each iteration. A smaller IP damping factor can be used can be used to allow a greater change in the IP model values. If a value of 1.0 is used, then the same damping factors will be used for both the resistivity and IP inversion routines. Normally a much smaller value of about 0.10 to 0.25 is used.
Visible=1
[IP damping settings\USE AUTOMATIC IP DAMPING FACTOR]
Display=Automatic IP damping factor
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Automatic estimate the IP damping factor from the Jacobian Matrix.
Visible=1
[IP damping settings\I.P. model damping factor]
Display=I.P. model damping factor
Type=Real
Min=0.05
Max=1
Default=0.25
User=0.25
Advanced=0
Description=Placeholder.
Visible=0
[IP damping settings\Use program estimate for I.P. model damping factor]
Display=Use program estimate for I.P.
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=Placeholder.
Visible=0
[Flatness filters]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Flatness filters
GroupName=Damping settings
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Flatness filters\Vertical to horizontal flatness filter ratio]
Display=Vertical to horizontal flatness ratio
Type=Real
Min=0.25
Max=4
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=By default, the same damping factor is used for both. However, if the anomalies in the subsurface are expected to be elongated vertically, you can force the program to produce models that are also elongated vertically by selecting a higher value (for example 2.0) for the ratio of the vertical to horizontal flatness filter. For anomalies that are elongated horizontally (e.g. in a sedimentary environment), choose a smaller value (e.g. 0.5).
Visible=1
[Flatness filters\Use diagonal filter]
Display=Use diagonal filter
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. The normal roughness filter used has components in the x and z directions only. Thus, it has a tendency to produce structures aligned along the x and z directions. To reduce this effect, the roughness filter used can be modified so that it has components in the diagonal directions as well.
Visible=1
[Flatness filters\Diagonal filter weight]
Display=Diagonal filter weight
Type=Real
Min=0.2
Max=5
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=Diagonal filter weight.
Visible=0
[L-curve]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=L-curve*
GroupName=Damping settings
Advanced=1
Visible=1
Description=
[L-curve\Use L curve method]
Display=Use L-curve method*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. In this option, the optimum damping factor is automatically determined by the program using the L-curve method. This method essentially estimates the data misfit and the model roughness for a large range of the damping factor values
Visible=1
[L-curve\Use same norms in L curve method]
Display=Use same norms in L-curve*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=1
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. Only relevant if you use the 'Robust' or L1-norm method in the 'Data/model norm' setting. This option allows you to use the same L1-norm method within the L-curve routine or use the standard L2-norm method.
Visible=1
[L-curve\Allow damping factor in increase in L curve method]
Display=Allow damping factor to increase during iterations*
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. For rare cases where the damping factor increases after an iteration. This option allows the user to ensure it does not increase.
Visible=1
[Topography]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=Topography
GroupName=
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=
[Topography\Type of topographical modeling]
Display=Type of topographical modeling
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,2,4,5
Default=4
User=4
Advanced=0
Description=0=None, 2=uniform distorted FEM, 4=damped FEM, 5=FEM with inverse Swartz-Christoffel.
Visible=1
[Topography\Topography distortion damping factor]
Display=Topography distortion damping
Type=Real
Min=0.1
Max=2
Default=0.75
User=0.75
Advanced=0
Description=Damping factor that controls the degree of damping with depth.
Visible=1
[Topography\Type of topography trend removal]
Display=Type of topography trend removal
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,2
Default=2
User=2
Advanced=0
Description=0=Average,1=Least-squares,2=End to end. Option to remove the average elevation, a least-squares linear trend, or a straight line joining the first and last topography data points. If the ground surface along the survey line is generally flat except for a few points, use the Average option to subtract a constant value from the heights of the electrodes locations. If the survey points are on a slope, choose either a least-squares or an end-to-end straight line for the linear trend.
Visible=1
[Topography\Carry out topography modeling]
Display=Carry out topography modeling
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No.
Visible=0
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings]
Selectable=0
Selected=1
DisplayName=IP settings
GroupName=
Advanced=0
Visible=1
Description=Placeholder.
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Type of I.P. model transformation]
Display=Type of IP model transformation
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1,3
Default=3
User=3
Advanced=0
Description=0=None, 1=square root, 3=range bound. The 'Square-root' transformation ensures that the model IP is always positive but does not directly set an upper limit. The 'Range bound' method ensures that the model IP values are restricted to the upper and lower range set in the 'Parameter range' settings.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Use I.P. model refinement]
Display=Use IP model refinement
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. In many data sets, the resistivity data misfit has a much larger range than the IP data misfit. This causes the inversion routine to determine a more accurate resistivity model at the expense of the I.P. model that might show a much smaller change with each iteration. To overcome this problem, after calculating the optimum resistivity and I.P. model through a joint inversion, a separate inversion to minimize the I.P. data misfit alone by changing the model I.P. values only (and keeping the model resistivity values fixed) is carried out in each iteration.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Joint or separate I.P. inversion method]
Display=Joint or separate IP inversion
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=1=Separate, 0=Joint. If separate is chosen the IP model is kept fixed during the resistivity inversion, the resistivity model is then refined afterwards. This might be useful for noisy datasets to avoid fitting the noise in the IP model.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Use apparent IP data in resistivity inversion]
Display=Use IP data in resistivity inversion
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Yes, 0=No. If 1, the IP measurements are included in the resistivity model, if 0, they are ignored. This only applies if separate inversion is chosen in the option above.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Weight for I.P. data]
Display=Weight for IP data
Type=Real
Min=0.1
Max=10
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=In most data sets, the data misfit for apparent resistivity values have a much larger range than the apparent IP values. This causes the program to give a greater weight to reducing the data misfit for the apparent resistivity compared to the apparent IP This effect can be compensated by giving a greater weight to the IP data misfit.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Type of I.P. smoothness constraint]
Display=Type of IP smoothness constraint
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=1
User=1
Advanced=0
Description=1=Same as resistivity, 0=Different. Normally the program uses the same smoothness constraint for the I.P. inversion as that used for the resistivity inversion. This option allows you to use a different smoothness constraint for the I.P. inversion. If the 'Robust' (L1-norm) method is used for the resistivity inversion, this option allows you to use the 'Smooth' (L2-norm) method for the I.P. inversion. Selecting the L2-norm constraint for the I.P. model might be useful for some cases as it has a much smaller range compared to the resistivity values.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Apparent I.P. cutoff value]
Display=Apparent IP cutoff value
Type=Real
Min=300
Max=899
Default=899
User=899
Advanced=0
Description=IP data above this value will not be used in the inversion.
Visible=1
[IP (Induced Polarization) settings\Type of IP inversion method]
Display=Type of IP inversion method
Type=Discrete
OptionsList=0,1
Default=0
User=0
Advanced=0
Description=0=CONCURRENT,1=SEQUENTIAL.
Visible=0
